The Arctic Fox has a low surface area to volume ratio and thick fur to conserve heat in its cold environment, and the Cactus has shallow roots and spines for shade to conserve water in its hot environment. 
These adaptations can range from changes in physical structures, such as the shape and size of an animal's body or its fur or feathers, to behavioral changes, such as migration patterns or hunting strategies. <|endoftext|>
