The relationship between the golden ratio and the Fibonacci sequence comes from the fact that the ratio of any two adjacent numbers in the Fibonacci sequence approaches the golden ratio as the sequence gets longer. In other words, if you take the ratio of the 10th and 9th terms of the Fibonacci sequence, it will be very close to the golden ratio. If you take the ratio of the 100th and 99th terms, it will be even closer.

This relationship between the Fibonacci sequence and the golden ratio can be seen in nature, art, and architecture. Many natural structures, such as seashells and pinecones, have proportions that follow the golden ratio, and many famous pieces of art and architecture have dimensions that are based on the golden ratio as well.

