How do scientists determine whether something is a solid, liquid, or gas?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
The state of matter of a substance is determined by the arrangement and motion of its constituent particles. In a solid, these particles are tightly packed together and vibrate in place; in a liquid, they are still close together but can flow past one another; and in a gas, they are widely spaced and move freely. The specific conditions of temperature and pressure also play a role in determining the state of matter. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
