How can I use C# code to calculate the surface area of a 3D square prism, where each side measures 16 centimeters in length? I want to provide the final answer in square centimeters.
Here's the C# code I came up with:
int sideLength = 16;
int surfaceArea = 6 * (sideLength * sideLength);
Console.WriteLine("The surface area of the 3D square prism is {0} square centimeters.", surfaceArea);
Is there a more efficient way to write this code?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Your code looks good and efficient. One possible improvement is to use the Math.Pow method to calculate the square of the side length instead of multiplying it by itself. Here's the updated code:
int sideLength = 16;
int surfaceArea = 6 * (int)Math.Pow(sideLength, 2);
Console.WriteLine("The surface area of the 3D square prism is {0} square centimeters.", surfaceArea);
This code uses the Math.Pow method to calculate the square of the side length, which is then multiplied by 6 to get the total surface area. The result is then cast to an integer to remove any decimal places. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
