<|user|>
How do I convert a string to int in C#?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
To convert a string to an int in C#, you can use the int.Parse or int.TryParse methods. The int.Parse method throws an exception if the string cannot be converted to an int, while int.TryParse returns a boolean value indicating whether the conversion was successful or not. Here's an example code:

string str = "123";
int num;

// Using int.Parse
num = int.Parse(str);
Console.WriteLine(num);

// Using int.TryParse
if (int.TryParse(str, out num))
{
 Console.WriteLine(num);
}
else
{
 Console.WriteLine("Conversion failed.");
}

In this code, the string "123" is first stored in the str variable. The int.Parse method is used to convert the string to an int, which is then stored in the num variable and printed to the console. The int.TryParse method is also used to convert the string to an int, but it first checks if the conversion is successful by returning a boolean value. If the conversion is successful, the resulting int value is stored in the num variable and printed to the console. Otherwise, the "Conversion failed." message is printed. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>


