<|fim_suf|>, LEFT JOIN, and RIGHT JOIN are different types of SQL joins that allow you to combine data from multiple tables based on common columns.

INNER JOIN returns only the rows where there is a match in both tables being joined. In other words, it only returns the rows where the join condition is true for both tables. It's useful when you only want to retrieve data that appears in both tables.

LEFT JOIN returns all the rows from the left table and the matching rows from the right table. If there is no match in the right table, NULL values are returned. It's useful when you want to retrieve all the data from one table and only the matching data from another table.

RIGHT JOIN is similar to LEFT JOIN but returns all the rows from the right table and the matching rows from the left table. If there is no match in the left table, NULL values are returned. It's useful when you want to retrieve all the data from one table and only the matching data from another table.

In general, you should use INNER JOIN when you only want to retrieve data that appears in both tables, LEFT JOIN when you want to retrieve all the data from one table and only the matching data from another table, and RIGHT JOIN when you want to retrieve all the data from one table and only the matching data from another table in a reversed order. Of course, the specific join type you use will depend on the particular requirements of your query. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>


