<|fim_suf|>, a stack and a queue are both abstract data types that allow you to store and retrieve elements. The main difference between a stack and a queue is the order in which elements are added or removed.

In a stack, the last element added is the first one to be removed, this is known as Last-In-First-Out (LIFO) behavior. This can be thought of as a stack of plates where the last plate added is the first one that can be taken off.

On the other hand, in a queue, the first element added is the first one to be removed, this is known as First-In-First-Out (FIFO) behavior. This can be thought of as a line of people waiting for a bus where the person who arrived first is the first one to board the bus.

Both stacks and queues have their own unique use cases in programming depending on the situation. In general, stacks are used when we need to keep track of a hierarchy of items or undo/redo functionality, while queues are used when we need to process items in a specific order such as processing incoming messages in a messaging app.

Do you have any more questions about data structures? <|endoftext|>
<|end|>


