What is the difference between a scientific theory and a hypothesis?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
A scientific hypothesis is a proposed explanation for a phenomenon, based on limited evidence or observations. It is essentially an educated guess that needs to be tested and refined through further experiments before it can be accepted as a theory. A scientific theory, on the other hand, is a comprehensive explanation for a natural phenomenon that is supported by a large body of empirical evidence and has been repeatedly tested and validated over time by multiple researchers. Theories are generally considered to be the highest level of scientific knowledge, and they can be used to make predictions about future observations. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
