<|user|>
Can you explain the difference between deductive and inductive reasoning?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Deductive reasoning starts from a general principle or theory and applies it to a specific case. If the principle is true, then the conclusion must also be true. Inductive reasoning starts from specific observations or examples and uses them to form a general principle or hypothesis. The conclusion is probable, but not guaranteed to be true. An example of deductive reasoning would be: "All men are mortal. Socrates is a man. Therefore, Socrates is mortal." An example of inductive reasoning would be: "Every time I've seen a swan, it has been white. Therefore, all swans must be white." <|endoftext|>
<|end|>


