<|user|>
How do vaccines work?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Vaccines work by stimulating the body's immune system to recognize and fight off a particular disease. They contain small amounts of weakened or inactivated viruses or bacteria that are similar to the disease-causing agent, but not strong enough to cause illness. When the vaccine is injected into the body, the immune system recognizes these foreign particles and creates antibodies to destroy them. If the person is later exposed to the actual disease, their immune system is already primed to attack it, preventing or minimizing the severity of the illness. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>


