<|user|>
Can you explain what is object-oriented programming and how it differs from procedural programming?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Object-oriented programming (OOP) is a programming paradigm that focuses on objects and their interactions to solve complex problems. In OOP, an object is an instance of a class, which can have its own properties (data) and methods (functions).

Procedural programming, on the other hand, is a programming paradigm in which a program is structured around procedures (also known as routines or subroutines), which are sets of instructions that perform specific tasks.

The key difference between OOP and procedural programming is that in OOP, programs are organized around objects and their interactions, whereas in procedural programming, programs are organized around procedures and the sequence of steps to be followed.

OOP provides several benefits over procedural programming, such as encapsulation (hiding the details of an object's implementation), inheritance (allowing classes to inherit properties and methods from other classes), and polymorphism (allowing objects to take on multiple forms). These features make it easier to write, maintain, and extend complex programs. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>


