<|user|>
What will be the output of the following C# code and why?
```csharp
int x = 3;
switch(x) {
 case 1:
 Console.WriteLine("One");
 break;
 case 2:
 Console.WriteLine("Two");
 break;
 default:
 Console.WriteLine("Default");
 break;
}
```
Explain how the "switch" statement works in C# and what is the value of x in this code. Provide reasoning for the expected output of the code.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
The value of `x` is `3`. 
In C#, the `switch` statement is used to select one of many code blocks to be executed. It works by evaluating the expression provided in the parentheses after the `switch` keyword and then executing the code block associated with the first `case` label that matches the value of the expression. If no `case` label matches the expression, the code block associated with the `default` label is executed (if it exists).
In this code, since `x` is equal to `3`, none of the `case` labels match its value. Therefore, <|fim_mid|>the code block associated with the `default` label is executed<|fim_eom|>

