<|fim_suf|>, the formula takes into consideration the fact that a leap year occurs every four years, with the exception of years divisible by 100 but not by 400. This means that there are 97 leap years in every 400-year cycle, so the formula adjusts for this by adding an extra day to certain calculations.

Regarding non-standard Gregorian calendar cycles, the formula assumes that the Gregorian calendar was adopted on October 15, 1582. Before that date, different countries used different calendars, and some countries continued to use their old calendars for several centuries after the adoption of the Gregorian calendar. If you need to handle dates before 1582 or for countries that use different calendars, you will need to adjust the formula accordingly.

Overall, Zeller's congruence formula is a useful tool for calculating the day of the week for a given date, taking into account both leap years and non-standard calendar cycles. <|endoftext|>
