<|end|>
<|asst|>
Climate change can affect ocean currents in a number of ways. As temperatures warm, ice sheets and glaciers melt, causing an increase in freshwater input into the oceans. This can disrupt the circulation of ocean currents, particularly in the North Atlantic, <|fim_suf|>, including increased frequency of extreme weather events like hurricanes, floods, and droughts. Furthermore, changes in ocean currents can also lead to alterations in marine ecosystems, affecting everything from plankton to fish populations. <|endoftext|>
