<|user|>
Can you explain the concept of dark matter and its importance in cosmology?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Dark matter is a hypothetical form of matter that is thought to make up about 85% of the total matter in the universe, but we cannot see or directly detect it. Its existence is inferred from its gravitational effects on visible matter, such as stars and galaxies. Dark matter plays a crucial role in cosmology because it helps to explain the observed structure of the universe, such as the way galaxies cluster together. Without dark matter, our current understanding of the universe would need significant revisions. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>




