In other words, the limit of the difference quotient used to calculate the derivative is equal to the derivative itself. This limit is often denoted using the symbol "f'(x)", where f is the function and x is the point at which the derivative is being evaluated.

So, essentially the derivative of a function is the limit of the difference quotient used to calculate the derivative as the interval over which it is calculated approaches zero. 

This relationship between limits and derivatives is one of the key concepts in calculus, and it plays an important role in many applications, including optimization problems, curve sketching, and differential equations. <|endoftext|>
