<|fim_suf|>, you can plot multiple lines on the same plot in MATLAB. To plot multiple lines with different colors, you can specify a color for each line using the `'Color'` property of the `plot` function. Here's an example:

Suppose you have two sets of data in the same Excel table format as before, stored in two different sheets within the same workbook. You can import the data into MATLAB and create a line plot of both sets of data on the same plot as follows:

```
% Import the first set of data from Sheet1
data1 = readtable('your_file_name.xlsx', 'Sheet', 'Sheet1');
x1 = data1.Column_1;
y1 = data1.Column_2;

% Import the second set of data from Sheet2
data2 = readtable('your_file_name.xlsx', 'Sheet', 'Sheet2');
x2 = data2.Column_1;
y2 = data2.Column_2;

% Create a new figure and plot both sets of data on the same plot
figure
hold on
plot(x1, y1, 'Color', 'red')
plot(x2, y2, 'Color', 'blue')

% Add axis labels and a title
xlabel('X-axis label')
ylabel('Y-axis label')
title('Title of the plot')

% Add a legend to differentiate between the two lines
legend('Line 1', 'Line 2')
```

This will create a new figure with a line plot of both sets of data on the same plot, where `y1` is plotted against `x1` in red and `y2` is plotted against `x2` in blue. You can customize the plot further by adding additional features using MATLAB functions. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>


