What is the average age of white dwarf stars in the Milky Way star cluster mentioned in the following table from the astronomy database?
Table: Star_Clusters
| ID | Name | White_Dwarf_Ages |
|----|------|-----------------|
| 1 | M1 | 5-10 million |
| 2 | M2 | 8-12 million |
| 3 | M3 | 12-15 million |
SQL query:
SELECT AVG(CAST(SUBSTRING_INDEX(White_Dwarf_Ages, '-', -1) AS UNSIGNED)) AS Average_Age
FROM Star_Clusters
WHERE Name = 'M1';
<|end|>
<|asst|>
The average age of white dwarf stars in the Milky Way star cluster named M1 is between 5 and 10 million years. This information is obtained from the following SQL query:
SELECT AVG(CAST(SUBSTRING_INDEX(White_Dwarf_Ages, '-', -1) AS UNSIGNED)) AS Average_Age
FROM Star_Clusters
WHERE Name = 'M1'; <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
<|user|>
What is the significance of white dwarf stars in astronomy?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
White dwarf stars are important in astronomy as they represent the final stage in the evolution of low- to intermediate-mass stars. They are extremely dense, with a mass comparable to that of the Sun but a size similar to that of the Earth. This creates a high gravitational field, which can cause interesting phenomena such as gravitational lensing and binary star interactions. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
<|user|>
How does the mass of a white dwarf star affect its properties?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
The mass of a white dwarf star has a significant impact on its properties. The more massive the white dwarf, the smaller its radius will be due to the increased gravitational pull. Additionally, <|fim_suf|>, white dwarf stars have exhausted their nuclear fuel and are no longer capable of undergoing fusion reactions. They are supported against gravitational collapse by electron degeneracy pressure, which arises from the exclusion principle governing the behavior of electrons in a confined space. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
<|user|>
What is the Chandrasekhar limit?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
The Chandrasekhar limit is the maximum mass that a stable white dwarf star can have before it collapses under its own gravity and becomes a supernova. This limit is approximately 1.4 times the mass of the Sun, beyond which point electron degeneracy pressure is not enough to counteract the force of gravity. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>


<|fim_mid|>more massive white dwarfs have higher surface temperatures and shorter lifetimes than less massive ones. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
<|user|>
Can white dwarf stars undergo nuclear fusion?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
No<|fim_eom|>

