Yes, you can modify the code to filter the objects based on their printing technology. Here's an updated Python code that takes a `technology` parameter and returns a list of objects that can be printed using that technology:
```python
class Object:
 def __init__(self, name, technology, printing_time, cost, material, complexity):
 self.name = name
 self.technology = technology
 self.printing_time = printing_time
 self.cost = cost
 self.material = material
 self.complexity = complexity

def get_printable_objects_by_technology(objects, technology):
 return [obj for obj in objects if obj.technology == technology]

# Create a list of 3D printable objects
objects = [
 Object("Phone Case", "FDM", 2, 5, "PLA", 1),
 Object("Keychain", "SLA", 1, 10, "Resin", 2),
 Object("Bottle Opener", "SLS", 3, 15, "Nylon", 3),
 Object("Vase", "FDM", 4, 20, "PLA", 4),
 Object("Chess Set", "SLA", 5, 30, "Resin", 5),
 Object("Lampshade", "SLS", 6, 40, "Nylon", 6)
]

# Get objects that can be printed using FDM technology
fdm_objects = get_printable_objects_by_technology(objects, "FDM")
for obj in fdm_objects:
 print(f"{obj.name} ({obj.technology}): printing time = {obj.printing_time} hours, cost = ${obj.cost}, material = {obj.material}")
```
In this modified code, I added a function `get_printable_objects_by_technology()` that takes a list of objects and a `technology` parameter and returns a filtered list of objects that can be printed using the specified technology. The function uses a list comprehension to filter the objects based on their technology attribute. You can call this function with the desired technology and print the resulting list of objects.

I hope this helps! Let me know if you have any more questions. <|endoftext|>
