<|fim_suf|>, you can simplify the code by removing the last else statement since it is not necessary. If the first condition is not met, then there is no need to check the second condition. Here's the updated code:

```csharp
using System;
class Program
{
 static void Main(string[] args)
 {
 double angle1, angle2, angle3;
 Console.WriteLine("Enter the three angles of the triangle:");
 angle1 = double.Parse(Console.ReadLine());
 angle2 = double.Parse(Console.ReadLine());
 angle3 = double.Parse(Console.ReadLine());

 // Check if the difference between any two angles is less than 180 degrees and the sum of any two angles is greater than the third angle
 if (angle1 + angle2 + angle3 == 180 && angle1 + angle2 > angle3 && angle1 + angle3 > angle2 && angle2 + angle3 > angle1)
 {
 Console.WriteLine("The angles form a triangle.");
 }
 else
 {
 Console.WriteLine("The angles do not form a triangle.");
 }

 Console.ReadLine();
 }
}
```

This code still checks if the difference between any two angles is less than 180 degrees and if the sum of any two angles is greater than the third angle. However, instead of having an extra else statement, it combines all three conditions into one if statement. <|endoftext|>
