<|fim_suf|>, in the sentence "He likes ice cream," the singular subject "he" agrees with the singular verb "likes". <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
<|user|>
What happens when the subject and verb do not agree in number?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
When the subject and verb do not agree in number, it can result in grammatical errors that can make the sentence difficult to understand. For example, in the sentence "They goes to the store," the plural subject "they" does not agree with the singular verb "goes", resulting in a grammatical error. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
<|user|>
Are there any exceptions to the subject-verb agreement rule?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Yes, there are some exceptions to the subject-verb agreement rule. One exception is with collective nouns, which are nouns that refer to a group of individuals. Collective nouns can take either a singular or plural verb depending on whether the group is seen as a single entity or as individuals. For example, in the sentence "The team is playing well," the collective noun "team" takes a singular verb because the team is seen as a single entity. However, in the sentence "The team are arguing among themselves," the collective noun "team" takes a plural verb because the team is seen as individuals. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
<|user|>
Can you provide another example of a collective noun?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Sure, another example of a collective noun is "family". In the sentence "My family is going on vacation," the collective noun "family" takes a singular verb because the family is seen as a single entity. However, in the sentence "My family are all very different," the collective noun "family" takes a plural verb because the family members are seen as individuals. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>


