<|user|>
Can you explain the difference between a stack and a queue in computer science?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
A stack and a queue are two common data structures used in computer science. A stack is a LIFO (Last-In-First-Out) structure, meaning that the last element added to the stack will be the first one removed. This can be thought of as a stack of plates - the last plate added will be the one on top and the first one removed. 

On the other hand, a queue is a FIFO (First-In-First-Out) structure, meaning that the first element added to the queue will be the first one removed. This can be thought of as a line at a store - the first person in line will be the first one served.

Both data structures have their own specific use cases depending on the problem being solved. For example, stacks are often used in programming languages to manage function calls and recursive algorithms, while queues are commonly used in scheduling and buffering. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>


