Can you explain the concept of dark matter and its significance in astrophysics?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Dark matter is a type of matter that is hypothesized to exist due to its effects on gravitational forces. It does not emit, absorb, or reflect light, making it invisible to current telescopes. Its existence has been inferred through observations of the motion of galaxies and clusters of galaxies. The significance of dark matter in astrophysics is that it makes up a significant portion of the total matter in the universe, but its exact composition and properties are still unknown. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
<|user|>
How do scientists determine the age of fossils?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Scientists determine the age of fossils using a variety of techniques, including relative dating and absolute dating. Relative dating involves comparing the ages of different rock layers and fossils within those layers to determine their relative order of formation. Absolute dating methods involve measuring the decay of radioactive isotopes in the fossil or surrounding rocks. By comparing the amount of the parent isotope to its decay products, <|fim_mid|>scientists can calculate the age of the fossil. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
