<|user|>
What is the difference between a virus and a worm in computer security?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
A virus is a type of malware that spreads by attaching itself to other files or programs and replicating when they are executed. Viruses require human interaction to spread, such as opening an infected email attachment or downloading a malicious software update. Worms, on the other hand, are self-replicating programs that do not require human interaction to spread. They can propagate across networks and systems automatically, exploiting vulnerabilities in software and hardware to infect as many devices as possible. While viruses typically cause damage to individual computers, worms can have much wider impact by spreading rapidly and causing widespread disruption to networks and systems. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>


