<|user|>
How does transfer learning work in deep learning?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Transfer learning involves taking a pre-trained model that has already learned useful features on one task and adapting it to perform a different but related task. By using a pre-trained model as a starting point, the neural network can leverage its knowledge and save significant time and resources. The strategy is particularly useful when the new dataset is small or has limited variation compared to the original data used to train the pre-trained model. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
<|user|>
What is the difference between overfitting and underfitting in machine learning?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Overfitting occurs when a machine learning model performs well on the training data but poorly on the test data because it has learned to memorize the training data rather than generalizing to new data. Underfitting, on the other hand, refers to a model that is too simple and does not capture the underlying patterns in the data, leading to poor performance on both the training and test sets. Both overfitting and underfitting can be addressed by adjusting the model complexity, regularization techniques, or balancing the training data. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>


