<|user|>
Can you explain the difference between supervised and unsupervised learning in machine learning?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Sure, supervised learning involves training a model on labeled data where the desired output is known. The goal is to learn a function that can map inputs to outputs accurately. Unsupervised learning, on the other hand, involves training a model on unlabeled data, with no predefined output. The objective is to discover patterns, structures, and relationships within the data. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
<|user|>
What is an activation function in neural networks?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
An activation function is a curve that maps the input values to output values needed for neural networks to model complex non-linear relationships. It introduces non-linearity into the system by transforming the weighted sum of the inputs from the previous layer with a non-linear function. Examples of activation functions include sigmoid, <|fim_mid|>ReLU (rectified linear unit)<|fim_eom|>

