<|user|>
Can you explain the difference between abstract classes and interfaces in Java?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Sure, <|fim_suf|>, an abstract class is a class that cannot be instantiated, but can be subclassed. It can contain both abstract and non-abstract methods, as well as instance variables. Abstract methods are those that do not have an implementation in the abstract class and must be implemented by subclasses.

On the other hand, an interface in Java is a collection of abstract methods and constants, but it cannot have instance variables or concrete methods. A class can implement multiple interfaces, but can only extend one class (including abstract classes). 

The main difference between abstract classes and interfaces is that a class that extends an abstract class can inherit its state and behavior, while a class that implements an interface only promises to provide implementations for the methods defined in the interface.

For example, let's say we have an Animal abstract class with an abstract method called makeSound(), and a Bird class that extends Animal and provides an implementation for makeSound(). We can also create a Flyable interface with an abstract method called fly() that is implemented by a Butterfly class.

```java
public abstract class Animal {
 public abstract void makeSound();
}

public class Bird extends Animal {
 public void makeSound() {
 System.out.println("Chirp chirp!");
 }
}

public interface Flyable {
 public void fly();
}

public class Butterfly implements Flyable {
 public void fly() {
 // Code to make the butterfly fly
 }
}
```

Here, the Bird class extends the Animal abstract class and overrides the makeSound() method. The Butterfly class implements the Flyable interface and provides an implementation for the fly() method. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>


