<|user|>
Can you explain the concept of "herd immunity" and how it relates to vaccination?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Herd immunity refers to the protection that a community or population gains when a sufficient number of individuals within that group have become immune to a particular disease. This can be achieved through vaccination, as well as through natural infections. When enough people are immune, the spread of the disease is slowed or stopped entirely, protecting even those who are not immune.

Vaccination plays an important role in achieving herd immunity by increasing the number of immune individuals within a population. When a large percentage of individuals are vaccinated against a specific disease, it becomes much more difficult for that disease to spread from person to person. This helps to protect individuals who cannot receive vaccinations, such as those with certain medical conditions or allergies, as well as those whose vaccinations may not have been effective.

Overall, herd immunity is an important public health goal that can help protect vulnerable populations from the spread of infectious diseases. Vaccination is a key tool in achieving herd immunity and reducing the overall burden of disease within a community. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>


