<|fim_suf|>, the first excerpt has a slow and deliberate pace, created by the repetition of "miles to go before I sleep." This repetition also adds a sense of urgency to the overall tone of the poem. The second excerpt, on the other hand, has a more relaxed pace, created by the use of enjambment (carrying over a thought from one line to the next). This creates a feeling of acceptance or resignation towards death.

Both poets employ specific techniques to create an impact on the reader. In the first excerpt, Robert Frost uses metaphor ("the woods are lovely, dark and deep") to evoke a sense of mystery and beauty. He juxtaposes this with the speaker's commitment to fulfilling his responsibilities, creating a sense of tension between duty and desire. In the second excerpt, Emily Dickinson personifies Death as a gentleman caller, treating it as a natural part of life rather than something to fear. This creates a feeling of comfort and acceptance towards death.

As for cultural or historical influences, both poems were written in the early 20th century, a time of great social and cultural change in America. Frost's poem reflects the tension between traditional values and modern progressivism, while Dickinson's poem reflects the growing acceptance of death as a natural part of life. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>


