We can also use the OpenGL library to visualize the weather patterns. Here is a code snippet that generates a random storm cloud:
#include 
#include 
void display() {
 glClear(GL_COLOR_BUFFER_BIT);
 glBegin(GL_QUADS);
 glColor3f(0.7, 0.7, 0.7);
 glVertex2f(-0.5, 0.5);
 glVertex2f(-0.5, -0.5);
 glVertex2f(0.5, -0.5);
 glVertex2f(0.5, 0.5);
 glColor3f(1.0, 1.0, 1.0);
 glVertex2f(-0.3, 0.3);
 glVertex2f(-0.3, -0.3);
 glVertex2f(0.3, -0.3);
 glVertex2f(0.3, 0.3);
 glColor3f(0.2, 0.2, 0.2);
 glVertex2f(-0.2, 0.2);
 glVertex2f(-0.2, -0.2);
 glVertex2f(0.2, -0.2);
 glVertex2f(0.2, 0.2);
 glEnd();
 glFlush();
}
void reshape(int w, int h) {
 glViewport(0, 0, w, h);
 glMatrixMode(GL_PROJECTION);
 glLoadIdentity();
 gluOrtho2D(-1, 1, -1, 1);
 glMatrixMode(GL_MODELVIEW);
}
int main(int argc, char** argv) {
 glutInit(&argc, argv);
 glutInitDisplayMode(GLUT_SINGLE | GLUT_RGB);
 glutInitWindowSize(500, 500);
 glutCreateWindow("Storm Cloud");
 glutDisplayFunc(display);
 glutReshapeFunc(reshape);
 glClearColor(0.2, 0.2, 0.2, 1.0);
 glutMainLoop();
 return 0;
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Regarding your second question, temperature and pressure play a crucial role in the formation and movement of storms and rains. In "The Tempest," Shakespeare describes a violent storm that was likely caused by a combination of high temperature and low pressure. A drop in pressure causes air to rise, and as it rises, it cools and forms clouds. The clouds then release their moisture as rain. In "The Perfect Storm," Junger describes a massive storm that was caused by the collision of two weather systems, one warm and one cold. The warm air rises and creates low pressure, which draws in the cold air from the surrounding areas. This creates a powerful system that can cause intense winds and heavy rains.
In summary, temperature and pressure are key factors in the formation and movement of storms and rains, and analyzing weather patterns in literary works like "The Tempest" and "The Perfect Storm" can help us understand the science behind these natural phenomena. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>


