Certainly! Here's an example using Python to retrieve the data and format it as an HTML table:
```shell
curl https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts | python -c "import json, sys, html;
data = json.load(sys.stdin);
table = '

| User ID | ID | Title | Body |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
';
for row in data:
 table += '| {} | {} | {} | {} |
'.format(html.escape(str(row['userId'])),
 html.escape(str(row['id'])),
 html.escape(row['title']),
 html.escape(row['body']))
table += '
';
print(table)"
```
This code will retrieve the data from the specified URL and format it as an HTML table with headers for each column. Each row of data will be displayed in a separate row of the table. The `html.escape` function is used to ensure that any special characters in the data are properly encoded for display in HTML. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
<|user|>
That's great! But what if the JSON file has nested data? How can we flatten it and display it in a table?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Good question! We can use the `json_normalize` function from the `pandas` library in Python to flatten the nested data. Here's an example code snippet:
```python
import pandas as pd
import requests

url = 'https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/comments'

response = requests.get(url)

data = response.json()

df = pd.json_normalize(data)

html_table = df.to_html(index=False)

print(html_table)
```
This code will retrieve the data from the specified URL, flatten any nested data using `json_normalize`, convert it to a Pandas DataFrame, and then convert the DataFrame to an HTML table using the `to_html` function. The resulting HTML table will display all of the columns in the DataFrame, including any columns that were originally nested within the JSON data. <|endoftext|>
