<|user|>
How does the human immune system respond to a viral infection?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
When a virus enters the body, it invades healthy cells and hijacks their machinery to replicate itself. The immune system responds by recognizing the foreign virus particles as antigens and triggering an immune response. 

The first line of defense is the innate immune system, which includes physical barriers like the skin and mucous membranes, as well as specialized cells like macrophages and natural killer cells. These cells can recognize and destroy infected cells, and can also release chemical signals called cytokines that attract other immune cells to the site of infection. 

The adaptive immune system comes into play if the innate immune system is unable to control the infection. This system produces antibodies that are specific to the virus, as well as T cells that can kill infected cells directly. Once the infection is cleared, memory cells are produced, which can protect against future infections with the same virus. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>


