I have a list of tasks in an Excel spreadsheet that I need to reset to their original values. Each task has a status column that needs to be set to "Not started". What formula or function in Excel can I use to start over and reset all the statuses to "Not started"?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
You can use the "IF" function in Excel to reset the status column to "Not started". Here's the formula you can use:
=IF(A2<>"Not started","Not started",A2)
Assuming the status column is column A and the first row with data is row 2, <|fim_suf|>, then it sets it to "Not started". <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
<|user|>
Can I use conditional formatting in Excel to highlight cells based on the value of another cell?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Yes, you can use conditional formatting in Excel to highlight cells based on the value of another cell. Here's how:
1. Select the cells that you want to apply the conditional formatting to.
2. Click on "Conditional Formatting" in the "Home" tab and select "New Rule".
3. In the "New Formatting Rule" dialog box, select "Use a formula to determine which cells to format".
4. In the "Format values where this formula is true" field, enter a formula that checks the value of the cell you want to base the formatting on. For example, if you want to highlight cells in column B if the corresponding cell in column A is greater than 10, you would enter the formula "=A1>10" (assuming your data starts in row 1).
5. Click on the "Format" button to specify the formatting you want to apply to the cells that meet the condition.
6. Click "OK" to close the "New Formatting Rule" dialog box.

Note that you can also use pre-defined rules in the "Conditional Formatting" menu to quickly apply common formatting options based on cell values, such as highlighting cells above or below a certain threshold. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>


