<|user|>
In addition to replacing "quick" with "fast" and "lazy" with "inactive" in the given paragraph, can you provide Ruby code to replace all occurrences of these words in a larger text corpus? Additionally, can the code be modified to only replace the words if they appear as whole words and not as part of another word (e.g., "quickly")?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Sure! Here's the Ruby code to replace all occurrences of "quick" with "fast" and "lazy" with "inactive" in a text corpus:
```ruby
text = "The quick brown fox jumped over the lazy dog."
text.gsub!(/bquickb/, "fast")
text.gsub!(/blazyb/, "inactive")
puts text
```
This will output:
```
The fast brown fox jumped over the inactive dog.
```
The `gsub!` method is used to replace all occurrences of the words "quick" and "lazy" with their respective replacements. The regular expression `b` is used to match word boundaries, so only whole words are replaced.
To use this code on a larger text corpus, you can simply replace the `text` variable with your own text. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
<|user|>
Is there a way to automate the process of scraping text from multiple web pages and replacing certain words like "quick" and "lazy" with their replacements using Ruby?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Yes, you can use Ruby libraries such as Nokogiri and Mechanize for web scraping. Here's some sample code that will scrape text from a website and replace occurrences of "quick" with "fast" and "lazy" with "inactive":

```ruby
require 'nokogiri'
require 'open-uri'
require 'mechanize'

agent = Mechanize.new

# Replace this URL with the URL of the website you want to scrape
page = agent.get("http://www.example.com")

# Get all the text on the page
text = page.search('body').text

# Replace occurrences of 'quick' and 'lazy'
text.gsub!(/bquickb/, 'fast')
text.gsub!(/blazyb/, 'inactive')

# Print the modified text
puts text
```

This code uses Nokogiri to parse the HTML of a webpage and extract all the text on it. Then, it uses Mechanize to simulate a browser and access the webpage. Finally, it replaces occurrences of "quick" and "lazy" in the text with their respective replacements using regular expressions, just like in the previous example. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>




