What is the difference between a virus and a bacterium?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Viruses and bacteria are both microscopic organisms that can cause diseases, but they differ in many ways. Bacteria are single-celled organisms that can live independently and reproduce on their own, while viruses are not considered alive and require a host cell to replicate. Bacteria can be treated with antibiotics, but viruses cannot be cured with antibiotics and generally require antiviral drugs or vaccines. Additionally, bacteria have a cell wall and can survive in a wide range of environments, while viruses do not have a cell wall and are not technically living organisms. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>


