<|fim_suf|>, and it allows applications to use more memory than is physically available. However, paging can also slow down performance since reading from and writing to the hard disk is much slower than accessing physical RAM.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
That's right! Additionally, there are different types of virtual memory allocation strategies used by operating systems. One common strategy is demand paging, where only the required pages are loaded into physical memory when they are actually needed. Another strategy is pre-paging, where the operating system tries to predict which pages will be needed soon and loads them into physical memory before they are actually requested by the application.

There are also different algorithms used to decide which pages should be swapped out of physical memory when space runs low. One such algorithm is the Least Recently Used (LRU) algorithm, which swaps out the least recently used page from physical memory.

Overall, virtual memory is an important technique for modern operating systems that allows them to efficiently manage memory usage and provide a larger addressable memory space for applications. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>


