Can you explain the difference between baking powder and baking soda, and when to use each one in a recipe?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Sure! While both baking powder and baking soda are used as leavening agents in baking, they work differently. Baking soda is a base, which means it reacts with acidic ingredients to produce carbon dioxide bubbles that cause baked goods to rise. On the other hand, baking powder contains baking soda and an acid, so it can be used in recipes without acidic ingredients. When liquid is added, the acid and baking soda in the baking powder react to produce carbon dioxide and cause the batter or dough to rise. Generally, baking soda is used in recipes with acidic ingredients like buttermilk or yogurt, while baking powder is used in recipes without these acidic ingredients. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>




