<|user|>
How does polymorphism work in OOP?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Polymorphism is another important concept in object-oriented programming, and it works hand-in-hand with inheritance. With polymorphism, you can define methods in the base class that can be overridden by the derived classes. This means that you can use a single method name for different implementations, depending on the type of object that calls it. There are two types of polymorphism: compile-time polymorphism and run-time polymorphism. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
<|user|>
What is encapsulation in OOP?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Encapsulation is the principle of hiding the implementation details of a class from other objects. It helps to protect the data within the class from being modified by external objects, ensuring that it remains in a consistent state. In OOP, encapsulation is achieved through access modifiers like private, public, protected, and internal. By using these access modifiers, you can control the visibility of the data members and methods of a class to other objects. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
<|user|>
Can you explain the difference between abstract classes and interfaces in OOP?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Abstract classes and interfaces are both used to define contracts that classes must adhere to. However, there are some key differences between them. An abstract class is a base class that cannot be instantiated, and it may contain concrete methods as well as abstract methods. A derived class must implement all the abstract methods of its base class, but it can also override the concrete methods if needed. An interface, on the other hand, is a set of method declarations without any implementation. A class that implements an interface must provide an implementation for all the methods declared in the interface. Additionally, a class can implement multiple interfaces but can only inherit from one abstract class. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>


