<|user|>
How can you modify an Excel table to be more accessible for students who use screen readers?
Here is an example of an Excel table with modifications for screen reader accessibility:
| Student Name | Math Grade | Science Grade | English Grade |
|--------------|------------|---------------|---------------|
| John | 90 | 85 | 95 |
| Sarah | 80 | 90 | 85 |
| Michael | 95 | 92 | 89 |
| Ashley | 87 | 91 | 93 |
To make this table accessible for screen reader users, the table headers should be properly labeled using the   tag. Additionally, table cells should be formatted to include row and column headers to help users navigate the table. Finally, alt text should be added to any images or graphs within the table to provide context for screen reader users. How can you implement these modifications in Excel? |
<|end|>
<|asst|>
To modify an Excel table to be more accessible for students who use screen readers, you can follow these steps:
1. Add header row: Add a header row to the table by selecting the first row and right-clicking to select "Insert" > "Insert Rows Above". 
2. Label headers: Label the headers in the header row using the   tag. 
3. Format cells: Format the cells to include row and column headers. To do this, select the data in the table, right-click and select "Format Cells". Under the "Alignment" tab, select "Wrap text" and "Shrink to fit". Then, under the "Border" tab, select "Outline" and "Inside". 
4. Add alt text: If there are any images or graphs within the table, add alt text to provide context for screen reader users. To do this, right-click on the image or graph and select "Format Picture". Under the "Alt Text" tab, add a description of the image or graph. 
By following these steps, you can make an Excel table more accessible for students who use screen readers. | <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
