Can you explain what is the difference between a list and a tuple in Python?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Yes, I can certainly explain that. In Python, both lists and tuples are used to store a collection of items, but there are some important differences between them.

One of the main differences is that lists are mutable, while tuples are immutable. This means that lists can be changed after they are created, whereas tuples cannot. For example, you can add, remove or modify elements in a list, but you cannot do this with a tuple.

Another difference is that lists are usually used for homogeneous data, i.e., a list of items that are all of the same type, such as a list of integers or a list of strings. Tuples, <|fim_mid|>on the other hand<|fim_eom|>

