Certainly! A number is prime if its only factors are 1 and itself. In other words, it cannot be divided evenly by any other number besides 1 and itself. The numbers in the set {6, 9, 12, 15, 18} are not prime because they can be divided evenly by 2 (except for 9, which is already not prime), 3, or some other factor besides 1 and themselves. For example, 6 can be divided evenly by 2 and 3, <|fim_suf|>, let's try starting with 21. We can add 3 four times to get the set {21, 24, 27, 30, 33}. Again, all of these numbers are divisible by 3 and none of them are prime. <|endoftext|>
