<|end|>
<|asst|>
Well, one possible application is in coding theory, where prime numbers and divisibility play a crucial role. For example, in some encryption algorithms, prime numbers are used to generate keys that are difficult to crack. However, if we want to ensure that certain properties of the data being encrypted are preserved (such as divisibility by 3), we may need to use non-prime numbers instead. Another application is in number theory more broadly, where it can be useful to have sets of numbers that meet certain criteria (such as being divisible by 3 but not prime) for research purposes. <|endoftext|>
