<|fim_suf|>, "CustomerID", and "OrderDate". Can you provide the Excel code to perform a JOIN query between these two tables? 
[Excel Table: Customers]
| CustomerID | CustomerName |
|------------|--------------|
| 1 | John |
| 2 | Jane |
| 3 | Bob |
[Excel Table: Orders]
| OrderID | CustomerID | OrderDate |
|---------|------------|--------------|
| 1 | 1 | 2021-01-01 |
| 2 | 2 | 2021-01-02 |
| 3 | 1 | 2021-01-03 |
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Yes, you can use the VLOOKUP function in Excel to retrieve the customer name and order date from the two related tables. Here's the Excel formula you can use:
`=VLOOKUP(CustomerID,Customers,2,FALSE)&" "&VLOOKUP(CustomerID,Orders,3,FALSE)`
This formula will retrieve the customer name from the "Customers" table and the order date from the "Orders" table based on the CustomerID. You can replace "CustomerID" with the actual cell reference of the CustomerID in the "Orders" table.
For example, if the CustomerID in the "Orders" table is in cell B2, the formula will be:
`=VLOOKUP(B2,Customers,2,FALSE)&" "&VLOOKUP(B2,Orders,3,FALSE)`
This formula will return the customer name and order date separated by a space. You can modify the formula to suit your specific needs. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
<|user|>
Can you explain the difference between a virus and a worm?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Sure, a virus is a type of malicious software that attaches itself to a host file and infects other files on the system when the infected file is executed. A virus requires a user action to spread, such as opening an infected email attachment or running an infected program.
On the other hand, a worm is a self-replicating malware that spreads through computer networks without requiring any user action. Worms exploit vulnerabilities in network protocols and security weaknesses to spread from one computer to another.
Unlike viruses that need a host file, worms are standalone executable programs that can operate independently on an infected system. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>


