<|asst|>
Correlation refers to a relationship between two variables where they tend to change together, while causation refers to when one variable directly affects another and causes it to change. A correlation does not necessarily imply causation; just because two variables are related does not mean that one is causing the other. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
<|user|>
How do scientists determine the age of fossils?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Scientists use a variety of methods to determine the age of fossils, <|fim_suf|>, relative dating, and stratigraphic correlation. Radiometric dating relies on the decay of radioactive isotopes in the fossil, while relative dating compares the position of the fossil in rock layers to other fossils or events in geological history. Stratigraphic correlation involves comparing the rock layers surrounding the fossil to those in other locations with known ages. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
<|user|>
What is the concept of herd immunity?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Herd immunity refers to the protection of a population against a particular disease when a large proportion of individuals in the population have developed immunity to the disease, either through vaccination or previous infection. When enough individuals are immune, the spread of the disease is slowed or even stopped, reducing the risk of infection for vulnerable individuals such as those who cannot be vaccinated. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
