How is satire different from parody?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Satire and parody are both forms of humor that use irony, exaggeration, or ridicule to comment on societal issues, politics, or human behavior. However, the main difference between satire and parody is their intended effect. 
Satire aims to expose and criticize the flaws in society or individuals, whereas parody aims to imitate and mock a particular work or genre. Satire often has a serious tone and an underlying message, while parody is typically more lighthearted and meant to entertain. Additionally, satire can be directed towards a broader target, such as a social issue or institution, while parody is targeted at a specific individual or work. <|endoftext|>
