<|user|>
Can you explain the concept of quantum entanglement?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Quantum entanglement is a phenomenon where two particles become correlated in such a way that the state of one particle depends on the state of the other, regardless of how far apart they are. This means that when one particle is measured, it affects the state of the other particle instantaneously, even if they are separated by vast distances. This has been famously dubbed "spooky action at a distance" by Einstein. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
<|user|>
How does the internet work?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
The internet is a global network of computers and servers that are connected to each other through a system of routers and switches. When you type a website address into your browser, your computer sends a request to a server, which responds by sending back the requested information. This information is broken up into packets that travel over the internet using various protocols, such as TCP/IP, DNS, and HTTP. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
<|user|>
What is dark matter and why is it important?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Dark matter is a hypothetical form of matter that is believed to make up approximately 85% of the matter in the universe. It does not interact with light or other forms of electromagnetic radiation, so it cannot be detected directly. However, its presence can be inferred from its gravitational effects on visible matter, such as stars and galaxies. Understanding dark matter is important because it helps us understand the structure and evolution of the universe, and also provides clues about the nature of fundamental particles and forces. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
<|user|>
Can you explain how a neural network works?
